            {"type": "prose", "kick": "Measuring improvement",
             "h2": "How Do You <em>Track Progress</em> in Golf Coaching?",
             "blocks": [
                 ("h3", "Which Numbers Actually Matter?"),
                 ("p", "Fewer than Trackman offers. For most golfers the useful set is carry distance per club, smash factor, dispersion and start direction. Those four tell you whether you are striking it better and controlling it better, which is what lowers scores. Club path and face angle matter while you are fixing a shape problem and can be ignored the rest of the time. Chasing every metric at once is the fastest way to make a coaching programme feel like homework."),
                 ("h3", "How Quickly Should I Expect to Improve?"),
                 ("p", "Strike quality tends to move first, often within a month, because smash factor responds quickly to a better grip and better low point control. Dispersion follows over two or three months. Handicap moves last and least predictably, because scoring depends on short game, course management and a certain amount of luck as well as ball striking. Judging a programme by handicap alone after six weeks is unfair on everybody involved."),
                 ("h3", "What If I Cannot Practise Much?"),
                 ("p", "Say so at the baseline session and the plan changes accordingly. A golfer with twenty minutes a week gets a different plan to one with five hours, and pretending otherwise just produces guilt and a plan nobody follows. Short, focused practice works surprisingly well: ten minutes of putting on the <a href=\"short-game.html\">practice green</a> beats an hour of hitting drivers for most handicaps."),
                 ("h3", "Can I Pause or Change Coach?"),
                 ("p", "Yes to both. Golf goes quiet for some people over winter and busy for others, and the programme should fit your year rather than the other way round. The plan is matched to your goals as they move, so a winter block on technique and a spring block on scoring are the same coach with a different aim. Will Painter, <a href=\"meet-team.html\">Advanced PGA Professional</a>, teaches the sessions throughout, which is why a change made in November is still being tracked in May."),
                 ("p", "Golfers travel to the programme from Derby, Nottingham, Ripley, Heanor, Belper and across Derbyshire and Nottinghamshire. Sessions run seven days a week from 7am to 8pm, and every facility on site is covered or floodlit, so a monthly plan does not quietly stop working in November."),
             ], "alt": True},

